{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Bugs:\n1. A single global `pending` chain — serializes all trades.\n2. `states.set(event.tradeId, next)` before `applyToStore` succeeds — state commits even before persistence. A rejection pollutes the state, and subsequent events are based on the polluted state.\n3. No version validation: duplicate/old versions should return the current snapshot; gaps should reject; CREATED must be first at version 1; CANCELLED is terminal.\n4. No payload validation: notional must be a positive finite value, owner must be a non-empty string; AMENDED must leave a valid and complete state.\n5. No immutability defense: event payload is spread directly (`...event.payload`), and the input event isn't being validated as non-mutable? \"Inputs, callback values, returned state, snapshots must not expose mutable internal state.\" structuredClone of input is fine. reduceEvent returns an object; for CREATED it constructs a new object with only the specified fields — but event.payload.notional etc. are primitives, so no issue. AMENDED's spread copies the payload fields — should validate types (primitives) so no object references leak. Also, the event itself should not mutate state; cloning is already implemented.\n\nAlso, validation errors (TypeError) must reject without changing state.\n\nDesign: per-trade queues (a Map from tradeId to Promise chain). Validate synchronously inside the chain.\n\nBehavior spec:\n- project(event):\n  - Validate event shape: non-null object, type one of the three, tradeId? Required by spec? \"Invalid first event rejects\"; \"Malformed event rejects with TypeError.\" Require tradeId to be a non-empty string? Reasonable. version is a positive integer.\n  - CREATED: notional must be positive finite number, owner must be non-empty string.\n  - Chain per tradeId:\n    - state = states.get(tradeId)\n    - type CREATED: state must be undefined (else? \"invalid first event rejects\" — CREATED to an existing trade rejects; TypeError or a regular Error? Probably reject; use TypeError for duplicate creation? Hmm. \"Version gap or invalid first event rejects without changing state.\" — just reject. Let's use TypeError for malformed, and a custom error for version conflicts. Simply: reject with TypeError for malformed, and a custom Error for version mismatch? For \"CREATED for an already-created trade\" — treat as invalid event; use TypeError since it's a malformed event (first event invalid). Actually duplicate version: \"duplicate or old version returns current snapshot without persisting\" — that's for a version ≤ current. A new CREATED to an existing trade… ambiguous. Reject with TypeError? Better: if event.version <= current version → return snapshot (covers duplicate of version 1). CREATED with version > 1 to existing trade → gap/invalid → reject. I'll reject with TypeError(\"CREATED cannot be applied to an existing trade\").\n    - version must be current+1; if version === current → return snapshot (duplicate); if version < current → return snapshot (old); if version > current+1 → reject with a gap error.\n    - CANCELLED terminal: if state.status === \"cancelled\" and new event with version current+1 → reject.\n    - AMENDED: payload may have notional and/or owner; after applying, notional must be positive finite, owner must be non-empty string. AMENDED's payload must not contain other keys? \"May replace either field\" — if other keys present, ignore or reject? Let's validate: each specified field must be valid; reject if extra keys? Safer to reject with TypeError on unknown keys? Spec says \"may replace either field but must leave a valid, complete state.\" Only accept notional/owner keys (if present, must be valid values); ignore unknown keys? To keep it simple and safe, validate known keys and reject on unknown? Hmm, risk: committed tests may test something. Let me first read the committed test files.\n\n    - AMENDED on a non-existent trade → reject (version gap: current undefined, version must be 1 but type isn't CREATED → invalid).\n    - Next, compute next = reduceEvent; state = next; await applyToStore(tradeId, copy(next), clone(event)); if rejected → restore previous state (don't set, or revert), and rethrow to the project caller. Must not pollute subsequent work: after rejection, subsequent events on that trade should be based on the previous state.\n\nOrder of validation: reject malformed with TypeError before touching the queue? But concurrency: validation can happen inside the chain after acquiring the per-trade lock. Even synchronous validation before the chain is fine (pure). Version check must be inside the chain because state may change.\n\nAlso \"different trades must be able to progress concurrently\" — per-trade chains.\n\n\"State commits only after applyToStore succeeds\" — set into states after await.\n\nConcurrency within a trade: chain: prevChain.then(...) ensures serial. If a prior project rejects, the next must still run — use a chain that doesn't store a rejected promise: `const result = chain.then(fn); statesChain = result.catch(()=>{});` pattern.\n\nImmutability: returned snapshot is structuredClone — good. applyToStore receives a clone — good. Input event: we clone for the store and read fields; reduceEvent for AMENDED spreads the payload — validate values are primitives (number/string) so no object references sneak into state.\n\nNow let me read the other files: committed tests, student-tests, INCIDENT.md.\n","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-89adaec469542919","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/event-projector.test.mjs"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4ed1ec7e7432966","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/student-tests.mjs"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9ba63265ce22354c","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-proj